  ## Highest priority

  1. Access-token validation accepts refresh tokens

  backend/app/auth/jwt.py:61 decodes the JWT but never verifies payload["type"] == "access". A valid refresh token can therefore authenticate against protected API endpoints.

  Also, missing or malformed sub values can produce an uncaught KeyError or ValueError, returning HTTP 500.

  Recommendation:

  - Require type == "access".
  - Validate sub explicitly.
  - Catch JWT, missing-claim, and conversion errors.
  - Return one consistent 401 response.

  2. Refresh-token rotation is not atomic

  backend/app/api/auth/services/refresh.py:39 performs:

  1. Read old token.
  2. Delete and commit.
  3. Generate new token.
  4. Insert and commit.

  If insertion fails, the old token has already been destroyed. Concurrent refresh requests can also race.

  Recommendation:

  - Lock the stored token row with FOR UPDATE.
  - Delete the old token and insert the replacement in one transaction.
  - Commit only after the complete rotation succeeds.
  - Add a random jti claim so tokens created within the same second are always distinct.

  3. Transaction ownership is fragmented

  Repositories commit internally throughout the backend, for example:

  - backend/app/db/repositories/routineRepository.py:25
  - backend/app/db/repositories/refreshTokenRepository.py:9
  - backend/app/db/repositories/userRepository.py:26
  - backend/app/db/repositories/workoutSessionRepository.py:178

  This prevents a service operation involving multiple repositories from being atomic.

  Recommendation:

  - Repositories should normally add, delete, flush, and query.
  - Services should own commit/rollback.
  - Add rollback handling to backend/app/db/dependencies.py:6 when request processing raises an exception.

  4. Signup has a uniqueness race

  backend/app/api/auth/services/signup.py:14 checks whether an email exists and then inserts it. Two simultaneous requests can pass the check; one then receives an uncaught database integrity error.

  Keep the friendly pre-check, but also catch the unique-constraint violation, roll back, and return HTTP 409.

  ## Major query optimizations

  5. Dashboard loads the same weekly data twice

  backend/app/api/dashboard/services/getDashboardHome.py:31 calls get_weekly_summary() and get_weekly_activity() separately. Both invoke backend/app/db/repositories/workoutRepository.py:12, loading the
  identical session/exercise/set graph twice.

  Recommendation:

  - Load weekly sessions once and calculate both responses from that collection; or
  - Prefer one SQL aggregation grouped by workout date.

  This is likely the best immediate performance improvement.

  6. Streak calculation loads the user’s entire workout history

  backend/app/db/repositories/workoutRepository.py:141 retrieves every completed session timestamp and creates a Python set. Its cost grows indefinitely.

  Recommendation:

  - Query distinct workout dates.
  - Stop once the first date gap is found.
  - Ideally implement the consecutive-date calculation in Oracle SQL using analytic functions.

  7. Calendar and statistics endpoints load full ORM graphs

  The calendar and 30-day statistics endpoints call backend/app/db/repositories/workoutSessionRepository.py:79, loading sessions, exercises, exercise definitions, and every set before aggregating in
  Python.

  Relevant consumers:

  - backend/app/api/workout/router.py:118
  - backend/app/api/workout/router.py:164

  Recommendation:

  - Calculate completed-set counts and volume using SQL SUM, COUNT, CASE, and GROUP BY.
  - Return compact projection rows rather than ORM entity graphs.
  - Only load detailed relationships for the session-detail endpoint.

  8. Dashboard quick routines return every routine

  backend/app/db/repositories/routineRepository.py:93 sorts and returns all user routines even though the dashboard presents them as “quick workouts.”

  Recommendation:

  - Add a bounded limit, likely 3–5.
  - Add an index on workout_sessions(user_id, routine_id, started_at) to support the usage aggregation.
  - Keep a separate unbounded method if another screen needs all routines.

  9. Exercise validation loads the complete exercise catalog

  Both routine and workout validation construct a set from ExerciseRepository.get_exercises(), including all polymorphic exercise columns:

  - backend/app/api/routine/router.py:39
  - backend/app/api/workout/router.py:358

  Recommendation:

  - Query only requested IDs: SELECT id WHERE id IN (...).
  - Compare the returned IDs against the request.
  - Avoid loading every exercise and subclass column.

  ## Workout correctness and efficiency

  10. Session finishing performs repeated flushes and rebuilds everything

  backend/app/db/repositories/workoutSessionRepository.py:117 flushes once per exercise. backend/app/db/repositories/workoutSessionRepository.py:182 deletes the existing exercise graph and reconstructs
  it.

  Recommendation:

  - Build related objects through ORM relationships and flush once.
  - Lock the session row while finishing to prevent duplicate concurrent finishes.
  - Consider updating existing rows when practical instead of delete/reinsert.
  - Use bulk insertion only after measuring; relationship construction with one flush may already be sufficient.

  There is also a correctness issue at backend/app/db/repositories/workoutSessionRepository.py:153: routine_exercise.rest_seconds or 90 converts a legitimate value of 0 to 90. Use 90 if value is None
  else value.

  11. PR generation needs stronger concurrency protection

  backend/app/db/repositories/workoutSessionRepository.py:231 reads previous maximum records and then inserts new records. Simultaneous operations can both decide they created the same PR.

  Recommendation:

  - Lock the relevant record scope or serialize session completion per user.
  - Define whether multiple historical PR rows with equal values are allowed.
  - Add an index such as (user_id, exercise_id, record_type, value) for maximum-value queries.

  ## Operational improvements

  12. SQL logging is always enabled

  backend/app/db/database.py:20 has echo=True. This increases log volume and can expose query parameters.

  Make it environment-controlled and disable it in production.

  13. Oracle pooling is layered

  The application creates an Oracle driver pool and passes pool.acquire into SQLAlchemy, which then uses its own pool unless configured otherwise.

  Recommendation:

  - When retaining the Oracle driver pool, configure SQLAlchemy with NullPool.
  - Alternatively, let SQLAlchemy manage pooling directly.
  - Add connection health checks such as pool_pre_ping.

  14. CORS configuration is invalid/risky for credentials

  backend/app/main.py:19 combines allow_origins=["*"] with allow_credentials=True.

  Configure explicit frontend origins from settings. This is safer and avoids inconsistent browser behavior around credentialed cross-origin requests.

  15. Time handling is timezone-naive

  The code mixes datetime.now(), date.today(), and Oracle timestamps, including:

  - backend/app/auth/jwt.py:18
  - backend/app/db/repositories/workoutSessionRepository.py:141
  - backend/app/api/workout/router.py:169

  Use timezone-aware UTC timestamps internally and convert to the user’s timezone only at API presentation boundaries. Otherwise workout dates, streaks, and token expiration can behave incorrectly after
  deployment in another timezone.

  16. No application test suite is present

  I found dependency-internal tests but no backend application tests. Priority coverage should include:

  - Refresh tokens cannot call protected endpoints.
  - Refresh rotation/replay/concurrency.
  - Only one active session per user.
  - Concurrent session finishing.
  - Routine recent-use ordering, including unused routines.
  - Dashboard query count.
  - User isolation on every repository query.
  - Deletion cascades and PR behavior.

  Suggested implementation order: JWT validation → transaction boundaries and refresh rotation → dashboard duplicate-load removal → streak/statistics aggregation → indexes based on measured Oracle
  execution plans.